## Frequently Asked Questions

### Is it normal to feel lonely in college?

Yes. College can involve major changes in relationships, routines, responsibilities, and living arrangements. Some students adjust quickly, while others need more time to find a sense of belonging. Feeling lonely does not mean you are failing at college or that you will always feel this way.

### Can therapy help with loneliness in college?

Therapy may help you understand why you feel disconnected, explore social anxiety or negative self-beliefs, develop coping strategies, and identify realistic ways to build meaningful relationships. The approach depends on your individual needs, and therapy is often most useful when combined with opportunities for supportive social connection.

### How do I know if my loneliness is affecting my mental health?

Consider whether loneliness is accompanied by persistent sadness, anxiety, withdrawal, changes in sleep or appetite, difficulty concentrating, or reduced interest in activities you normally enjoy. If symptoms are distressing, persistent, or interfere with daily life, consider talking with a qualified mental health professional.

### How can I make friends in college if I am shy?

Start with manageable interactions, such as greeting a classmate, asking about coursework, or attending a recurring activity related to your interests. You do not need to become outgoing overnight. If fear of judgment consistently prevents you from connecting, therapy may help you work through that anxiety.

### What if I have friends but still feel lonely?

Having friends does not always mean feeling understood or emotionally connected. You might want deeper conversations, more dependable support, or relationships in which you feel comfortable being yourself. Reflecting on what kind of connection you need may help you decide what to communicate or look for in your relationships.

### Should I go to my college counseling center or find a private therapist?

Either may be an appropriate starting point. A college counseling center may be convenient for students, while a private therapist may offer another option depending on access, scheduling, costs, and individual needs. Ask about services, availability, confidentiality, and whether the provider is a good fit for your concerns.

### Does Riaz Counseling offer online therapy for college students?

Riaz Counseling lists online therapy for people living throughout Texas, as well as in-person services in the Houston area. Contact the practice to confirm whether its current services and appointment options fit your needs.

## Conclusion

Loneliness during college can be difficult to explain. You may be surrounded by people and still feel disconnected, or you may want to make friends but find that anxiety, stress, homesickness, or uncertainty makes it hard to take the first step.

You do not have to judge yourself for feeling this way.

Building meaningful relationships takes time, and connection is about more than how many people you know. Small interactions, shared activities, existing relationships, and a routine that supports your well-being can all play a part.

If loneliness continues to affect your emotional health or everyday life, therapy may help you better understand your experience and identify practical ways forward.

**You do not have to wait until loneliness becomes overwhelming to seek support. You deserve relationships where you feel valued, understood, and able to be yourself—and taking one small step toward that connection is a meaningful place to begin.**

### Mental Health Disclaimer

This article is for educational purposes only and is not a diagnosis or a substitute for individualized mental health care. Loneliness can have many contributing factors, and a qualified professional can help you determine what support may be appropriate.

If you are in the United States and experiencing a mental health crisis or thoughts of suicide, call or text **988** for the Suicide & Crisis Lifeline. If you or someone else is in immediate danger, call **911**.

